& [PrimaryResearchAnchors]
  + NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 Revision 5 treats revocation as notification that continued key use is no longer recommended and separates notification from the relying party decision: https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/57/pt1/r5/final
  + NIST SP 800-57 Part 2 Revision 1 requires revocation capability and identifies CRLs compromised-key lists and online mechanisms as ways to communicate status including reason and time: https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/57/pt2/r1/final
  + RFC 5280 requires signed CRL structure issuer thisUpdate nextUpdate CRL number and authority-key identity while an empty revoked-certificate list means no listed unexpired certificate was revoked under that CRL scope: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5280.html
  + RFC 6960 distinguishes good revoked and unknown response states; thisUpdate is when status was known correct nextUpdate bounds availability of newer information producedAt is signing time and revocationTime is the revocation event: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6960.html
  + RFC 6960 also requires an authorized responder and local-time checks before relying on a response; signature validity alone cannot establish responder authority current reliability completeness or the present clock relation
  + Cantor therefore verifies snapshot and signature correspondence now while leaving authorization freshness completeness rollback prevention current-time evaluation and operative revocation truth to separately governed coordinates

